1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,440
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:04,380
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,320
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:14,960
PHILLIP ISOLA: So today, we're going

5
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:17,560
to talk about scaling laws.

6
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:22,740
And this lecture, we're at the final few weeks of this semester

7
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:25,320
so we're moving into territory which

8
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:29,200
is much more ongoing science.

9
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,080
I'm going to talk about recent papers and results that

10
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:34,440
are empirical in nature for which we don't

11
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,702
know if these results will really hold up,

12
00:00:36,702 --> 00:00:37,660
stand the test of time.

13
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:41,840
But this is the latest research on deep learning.

14
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:44,500
And a lot of the next few lectures will be of that flavor.

15
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,200
So what are scaling laws?

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,400
Why do we care about scaling in deep learning?

17
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:57,160
And the reason is that deep learning

18
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:58,960
is something that works really well

19
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:02,440
once you have a lot of scale, once you have a lot of data,

20
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:07,480
a lot of compute, a lot of flops, a lot of parameters,

21
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:10,000
big models on big data with large machines.

22
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,660
That's kind of the regime in which deep learning is

23
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,540
winning and doing very well.

24
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,860
And a big part of the story of deep learning

25
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:19,820
is how do you scale things to make efficient use

26
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,740
of the resources that you have.

27
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:25,340
And a remarkable thing that's been observed

28
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:29,500
is that, sometimes, the way that models behave as they scale

29
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:30,980
is quite predictable.

30
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:33,100
And so you can define these scaling laws, which

31
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,857
are predictions of if I double my compute,

32
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:37,940
how will the model behave?

33
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:41,460
If I double my model size, how will the model behave?

34
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:43,840
And if we can identify those types of laws,

35
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,340
then this will be really useful for planning our resources

36
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:50,000
and our extrapolations into the future.

37
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,800
We can predict what next year will bring.

38
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,717
How competent will be the models of next year?

39
00:01:53,717 --> 00:01:55,980
Well, if have a law of how does the performance

40
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,990
change as a function of data points,

41
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:02,490
then we can know how well the models will perform next year.

42
00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:05,470
And that's very useful for us to plan.

43
00:02:05,470 --> 00:02:09,190
So here is one example of a scaling law

44
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:10,669
that people have noticed.

45
00:02:10,669 --> 00:02:12,330
This is from an OpenAI blog.

46
00:02:12,330 --> 00:02:14,410
And on the x-axis, they're plotting year.

47
00:02:14,410 --> 00:02:15,870
And on the y-axis, they're plotting

48
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:20,930
the scale of the compute that's used to train each model.

49
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:24,508
So this is saying that as years go on,

50
00:02:24,508 --> 00:02:26,550
the amount of compute that we use to train models

51
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:31,030
is increasing as some kind of exponential,

52
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:34,030
or it may, in fact, be a power law in this case.

53
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:37,230
But basically, for every additional year that we go on,

54
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:41,870
we get a kind of big increase in the amount of compute

55
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:43,250
that we use in these models.

56
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:45,850
So this should be reminiscent of things like Moore's law.

57
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:49,110
So Moore's law said that we double the number of transistors

58
00:02:49,110 --> 00:02:52,710
in our machines every two years or however much it was.

59
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:56,710
And this scaling law says that every year--

60
00:02:56,710 --> 00:02:58,690
actually, every three months, I think,

61
00:02:58,690 --> 00:03:00,730
you double the amount of compute that you

62
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:02,610
use to train the models.

63
00:03:02,610 --> 00:03:04,953
And I think this law has actually been increasing.

64
00:03:04,953 --> 00:03:06,870
The slope is actually increasing a little bit,

65
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:09,290
so it's not quite even three months

66
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:11,550
now that it takes to double the amount of compute,

67
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:13,530
it might be even less.

68
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:16,730
See, this ends in 2018.

69
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:21,290
So deep learning, predictably, is using more and more scale

70
00:03:21,290 --> 00:03:22,610
as time goes on.

71
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:25,730
And now, we want to understand how that relates to the model

72
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:27,770
performance and at what scale will we achieve

73
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:31,410
what types of abilities.

74
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:35,210
So another motivation for studying these questions comes

75
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:38,330
from this really influential essay called "The Bitter Lesson"

76
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:39,310
from Richard Sutton.

77
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:41,470
So raise your hand if you've read this.

78
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:43,330
It's a one-pager.

79
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:44,870
So you should all take a look at it.

80
00:03:44,870 --> 00:03:49,050
It caused quite a splash in the field when it came out in 2019.

81
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:51,110
A lot of people really didn't like it.

82
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:52,602
A lot of people loved it.

83
00:03:52,602 --> 00:03:54,310
But I think there's a lot of truth to it.

84
00:03:54,310 --> 00:03:56,050
And it's an important lesson for us

85
00:03:56,050 --> 00:04:00,270
to understand and take to heart, despite some nuances and caveats

86
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:01,590
that you can add to it.

87
00:04:01,590 --> 00:04:05,890
So what Rich Sutton was saying is that-- he's an AI researcher.

88
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:08,550
He's been working in the field for a long time.

89
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:12,110
And what he observed is that all of our clever ideas

90
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:15,390
and algorithms, most of them don't really pan out.

91
00:04:15,390 --> 00:04:18,390
And the only thing that systematically pans out

92
00:04:18,390 --> 00:04:22,528
is designing simple algorithms that scale well.

93
00:04:22,528 --> 00:04:24,070
And so he's basically saying that you

94
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:27,590
need to think about how models scale as a function of resources

95
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:32,435
in order to identify the winning models because, as time goes on,

96
00:04:32,435 --> 00:04:34,310
whatever the ranking, whatever model is doing

97
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:36,970
better or worse than any other model, it doesn't really matter.

98
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:39,690
What matters is how they'll scale because, eventually,

99
00:04:39,690 --> 00:04:44,982
as time goes on, the one that scales better will win.

100
00:04:44,982 --> 00:04:46,550
So it was a little bit of a critique

101
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:48,930
of all of the clever algorithms in the field.

102
00:04:48,930 --> 00:04:51,950
No, it turns out that the really simple algorithms turn out

103
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:55,230
to scale better, and those are taking over.

104
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:58,040
So interesting essay, but it has really

105
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:01,200
led us to take scale quite seriously.

106
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:04,040
Of course, scaling laws and thinking

107
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,960
about how algorithms scale as a function of resources

108
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:08,440
is not at all new.

109
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:11,680
It goes back to the foundations of computer science.

110
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:14,120
So in Intro CS or in Algorithms, you

111
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:17,180
learn about asymptotic analysis, big O notation.

112
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:18,860
That's a scaling law.

113
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:25,440
That's saying how does my time complexity of an algorithm scale

114
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:27,743
as a function of the input complexity.

115
00:05:27,743 --> 00:05:29,160
Like if I have a graph, I'm trying

116
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:31,600
to find shortest path as a function of the number of nodes

117
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,728
in the graph, how does the model's behavior, how does

118
00:05:34,728 --> 00:05:36,020
the algorithm's behavior scale?

119
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:38,800
How long does it take to find the shortest path?

120
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,700
So this idea of asymptotic analysis,

121
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,060
looking at as resources or complexity

122
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:45,520
or as some parameter of the input

123
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:47,560
scales up, how does some property

124
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:52,720
of the model scale, that's a standard part of computer

125
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:53,720
science.

126
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:57,860
And it's very much something that you want in order

127
00:05:57,860 --> 00:05:59,360
to make predictions about the future

128
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:00,800
because things are scaling.

129
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,300
That's kind of the bottom line of this graph

130
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:05,187
here is that things are scaling.

131
00:06:05,187 --> 00:06:06,520
Eventually, they might saturate.

132
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,062
We might not have any more resources.

133
00:06:08,062 --> 00:06:10,060
But we're not really anywhere near that point,

134
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:13,400
so we need to be able to predict how things will scale.

135
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:19,140
So one of the fundamental questions

136
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:26,580
of the study of scaling laws is if I have a budget of compute

137
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:28,980
or a budget of hardware, how should I

138
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:34,380
optimally allocate that budget?

139
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:38,340
So, let's see.

140
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:41,460
What model should I train on, how much data, in order

141
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:43,360
to make the best use of my budget?

142
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:49,380
So here is a scaling law, another scaling law,

143
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:53,460
from OpenAI that they use to decide on how

144
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:56,240
to train the GPT-4 model.

145
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:59,920
So now, we have on the x-axis is the compute, and on the y-axis

146
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,960
is the performance of the model.

147
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:04,060
So performance is measured in bits per word.

148
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:07,160
So this is something related to the cross-entropy

149
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:09,240
of the next-word prediction.

150
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:12,600
So lower means that I'm making more accurate predictions.

151
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:16,520
And as I train my model with more compute, so more flops,

152
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:21,480
more steps of SGD, more gradients, more updates,

153
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:24,600
so compute is now measured in just flops on the hardware.

154
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:27,000
And that is related in a complicated way

155
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,652
to the number of tokens that I observe,

156
00:07:29,652 --> 00:07:30,860
the depth of the transformer.

157
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:32,260
All these things affect the compute,

158
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:33,860
but we're just measuring it all in flops,

159
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:35,610
like how many floating point operations do

160
00:07:35,610 --> 00:07:37,920
I do on my hardware?

161
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:40,000
So as I use more compute, of course,

162
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,920
I will get better models with lower loss.

163
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:49,640
And so 1 here is like a petaflop,

164
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,620
or I'm not entirely sure what the axis is,

165
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:53,860
but this is a lot, a lot, of compute.

166
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:58,700
And if you read this paper and saw that training GPT-4

167
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:00,940
cost $10 million or however much it

168
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,660
costs to do one training, like a petaflop of compute

169
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,560
was $1 million, let's say, then you might have thought,

170
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:09,340
well, obviously, they did a hyperparameter sweep,

171
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:11,940
and they tried a lot of model variations.

172
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:14,680
If it took $1 million to train the final model,

173
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:17,660
it must have taken $100 million to design it, right?

174
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,780
But that's not actually the case.

175
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:21,852
I don't know how much it took to design it or run

176
00:08:21,852 --> 00:08:23,560
the final model, but they did this trick,

177
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:26,980
which is they don't design it by sweeping hyperparameters

178
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:28,880
and testing models at this scale.

179
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:34,080
They design it at 1/1,000 the scale, or maybe in this case,

180
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:39,380
it's 10,000, 1/10,000 the scale.

181
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:42,580
So they do all of their design and hyperparameter sweeps

182
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:47,060
and ablations and model comparisons at small scale.

183
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:48,960
They do it at multiple small scales,

184
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:53,390
and they find this curve that will predict performance

185
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:55,270
as a function of compute, and then

186
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:57,150
extrapolate that curve to the scale

187
00:08:57,150 --> 00:08:59,190
that they'll train their final model at.

188
00:08:59,190 --> 00:09:02,430
And if they can predict that the final model will

189
00:09:02,430 --> 00:09:05,765
get such and such a score, they will

190
00:09:05,765 --> 00:09:07,390
choose the model that will get the best

191
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:09,310
score by running these curves for a bunch

192
00:09:09,310 --> 00:09:11,152
of different variations.

193
00:09:11,152 --> 00:09:13,110
Then they'll choose the model that they predict

194
00:09:13,110 --> 00:09:15,670
will have the lowest score at the skill

195
00:09:15,670 --> 00:09:17,070
that they actually care about.

196
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:20,008
And then they only train once at that massive scale.

197
00:09:20,008 --> 00:09:22,550
So that's the strategy that a lot of companies are using now.

198
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:24,750
Yeah, question in the back.

199
00:09:24,750 --> 00:09:28,310
AUDIENCE: My question is, is compute proportional to time?

200
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:30,490
Like, let's take into account the amount of time?

201
00:09:30,490 --> 00:09:33,550
Or is it something that's time-normalized?

202
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:34,570
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

203
00:09:34,570 --> 00:09:35,570
That's a great question.

204
00:09:35,570 --> 00:09:37,270
Is compute proportional to time?

205
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:44,670
So here, you're just kind of flattening all uses of compute

206
00:09:44,670 --> 00:09:46,170
into just flops.

207
00:09:46,170 --> 00:09:50,310
So flops could be allocated sequentially or in parallel.

208
00:09:50,310 --> 00:09:53,610
So compute does not have a direct relationship to time.

209
00:09:53,610 --> 00:09:57,310
So twice the compute can be twice the time,

210
00:09:57,310 --> 00:10:00,290
or it could be twice the number of GPUs in parallel.

211
00:10:00,290 --> 00:10:01,770
So this is just measuring compute

212
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:06,930
in terms of flops, which can factor into different mappings

213
00:10:06,930 --> 00:10:09,050
onto time.

214
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:10,090
Yeah.

215
00:10:10,090 --> 00:10:14,250
AUDIENCE: Is this graph for a constant amount of data?

216
00:10:14,250 --> 00:10:16,110
PHILLIP ISOLA: So I'll get to that.

217
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:18,000
Is this graph for a constant amount of data?

218
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,750
I don't know for this graph in particular,

219
00:10:19,750 --> 00:10:22,050
but usually, for scaling laws, we

220
00:10:22,050 --> 00:10:25,330
will try to say we're going to predict

221
00:10:25,330 --> 00:10:28,450
the performance at a given compute level

222
00:10:28,450 --> 00:10:30,830
for the optimal amount of data for that compute level.

223
00:10:30,830 --> 00:10:32,610
We'll optimize it for all other factors

224
00:10:32,610 --> 00:10:33,710
when we make one of these graphs,

225
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:35,293
but I'll show you that in more detail.

226
00:10:38,590 --> 00:10:40,490
And these types of scaling laws, they

227
00:10:40,490 --> 00:10:42,630
don't only appear for the loss.

228
00:10:42,630 --> 00:10:45,330
So bits per word is closely related

229
00:10:45,330 --> 00:10:48,290
to the loss function of next-character prediction.

230
00:10:48,290 --> 00:10:51,500
It's like the amount of information you--

231
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:53,750
the amount of uncertainty you have over the next word,

232
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:56,430
essentially, measured in bits.

233
00:10:56,430 --> 00:10:58,872
But what if we try to predict performance

234
00:10:58,872 --> 00:11:00,830
in terms of something more meaningful, like how

235
00:11:00,830 --> 00:11:04,510
many problems you pass on some math test or some benchmark?

236
00:11:04,510 --> 00:11:07,350
And it turns out that there are also fairly predictable trends

237
00:11:07,350 --> 00:11:09,690
in scaling laws for the downstream performance,

238
00:11:09,690 --> 00:11:12,085
not just for the loss.

239
00:11:12,085 --> 00:11:13,710
So a lot of scaling, a lot of research,

240
00:11:13,710 --> 00:11:16,710
is on predicting the loss on the training set

241
00:11:16,710 --> 00:11:18,330
as a function of resources.

242
00:11:18,330 --> 00:11:21,150
But you can also make scaling laws for test performance

243
00:11:21,150 --> 00:11:23,710
or for downstream performance, in other senses,

244
00:11:23,710 --> 00:11:27,380
according to other measures.

245
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:30,730
So this was capability on 23 coding problems.

246
00:11:30,730 --> 00:11:32,470
And again, you have this nice curve.

247
00:11:32,470 --> 00:11:34,950
And what I'll tell you is that these curves turn out

248
00:11:34,950 --> 00:11:40,270
to be power laws with respect to the underlying resource.

249
00:11:40,270 --> 00:11:44,870
So scaling laws are things that try

250
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:47,510
to predict some parameter of interest, so, oftentimes,

251
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:52,468
the test loss as a function of the amount of data you train on,

252
00:11:52,468 --> 00:11:54,760
the amount of compute you use, the number of parameters

253
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:58,480
in your model, these critical resources that you care about.

254
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:00,280
And what you're going to try to do

255
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:02,520
is select these resources-- these will inform you

256
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:05,560
on how to select these resources so as to get the lowest

257
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:07,013
possible test loss.

258
00:12:07,013 --> 00:12:08,680
So that's why we care about scaling laws

259
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:12,520
because they tell us what should the batch size be

260
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:17,360
that will achieve the lowest cost when I scale things way up,

261
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:21,620
the lowest loss when I scale things way up.

262
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:24,000
So for a given architecture, how much data, what model,

263
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:25,833
would be needed to get a target performance?

264
00:12:25,833 --> 00:12:30,320
If I know I need to be like 99% accurate on my self-driving car

265
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:34,980
assessment, then can I predict at what scale will I have that?

266
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:37,260
And that would be useful for regulatory bodies.

267
00:12:37,260 --> 00:12:40,560
I want to tell them in 2025, we'll have 99% performance,

268
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:42,520
and you can start planning around that.

269
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:45,388
So it's good for making predictions about the future

270
00:12:45,388 --> 00:12:46,180
and planning ahead.

271
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:50,060
It's also good for choosing parameters that maximize

272
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:53,540
performance at a given scale.

273
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:59,060
So generally, the kind of scaling laws we'll look at

274
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:02,720
are going to be of the form as a function of parameters.

275
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:06,560
And we'll look at number of-- as a function of resources.

276
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:08,620
So we'll look at N, the number of parameters,

277
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:11,160
and D, the number of data points.

278
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:12,700
So D is data points now.

279
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:13,840
It's not depth.

280
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:15,995
Just these terms are all overloaded.

281
00:13:15,995 --> 00:13:17,120
D could be a lot of things.

282
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:18,560
But here, D is not dimensionality.

283
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:19,220
It's not depth.

284
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:21,960
It's data points.

285
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:23,620
So we're going to try to find what

286
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:26,500
is the optimal number of parameters

287
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,100
and what is the optimal number of data points for a fixed

288
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:35,180
compute budget C. So C is going to be the flops that

289
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:41,340
are required to train a model of size N on a data set of size D.

290
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:45,900
So roughly, to train a model with N parameters

291
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:48,280
on a data set of size D, we'll have something

292
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:50,740
like N times D amount of flops.

293
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:52,740
It might be a little more complicated than that,

294
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:55,840
but every additional data point, I need proportionally more

295
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:57,520
flops, every additional parameter,

296
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:01,240
I need proportionally more flops.

297
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:04,200
So for a fixed compute budget, if I want

298
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:07,200
to minimize my performance--

299
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:12,340
sorry, I want to minimize my loss, so maximize performance.

300
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:13,520
There we go.

301
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:14,755
Then what should I set?

302
00:14:14,755 --> 00:14:16,880
How big should my model be and how many data points

303
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:18,500
should I train on?

304
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:19,920
So we can train a really big model

305
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,840
on a few data points for the same cost

306
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:24,107
as a small model on a lot of data points.

307
00:14:24,107 --> 00:14:24,940
So what should I do?

308
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:29,440
Should I train a huge trillion-parameter transformer

309
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:31,540
on 10 data points?

310
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:34,760
Or should I train a 1 billion-parameter transformer

311
00:14:34,760 --> 00:14:36,680
on a million data points?

312
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:40,820
Those might have roughly the same cost in terms of flops.

313
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,570
So the paper that really introduced or popularized

314
00:14:47,570 --> 00:14:49,650
the idea of scaling laws for neural nets

315
00:14:49,650 --> 00:14:52,930
was this one from Jared Kaplan and Sam McCandlish

316
00:14:52,930 --> 00:14:55,610
and folks at OpenAI.

317
00:14:55,610 --> 00:14:57,890
This team subsequently peeled off

318
00:14:57,890 --> 00:15:00,450
and formed the company Anthropic, but a few of them

319
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:01,270
stayed at OpenAI.

320
00:15:01,270 --> 00:15:03,730
So basically, the two big AGI players,

321
00:15:03,730 --> 00:15:09,050
or two of the big AGI players, is a schism of this team.

322
00:15:09,050 --> 00:15:10,810
And so the scaling laws are a huge part

323
00:15:10,810 --> 00:15:14,450
of the philosophy behind those two companies and also part

324
00:15:14,450 --> 00:15:16,610
of their success, I would say.

325
00:15:16,610 --> 00:15:20,790
So they studied scaling laws in 2020 or so,

326
00:15:20,790 --> 00:15:24,650
or maybe this is like 2018, for autoregressive transformer

327
00:15:24,650 --> 00:15:27,210
models, so language models with a transformer.

328
00:15:27,210 --> 00:15:29,130
And they tried to solve the equation

329
00:15:29,130 --> 00:15:31,010
I had on the last slide.

330
00:15:31,010 --> 00:15:33,770
How big should the model be, and how many data points

331
00:15:33,770 --> 00:15:37,910
should we train it on if we have this compute budget?

332
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:40,490
Maybe they have a billion dollars'

333
00:15:40,490 --> 00:15:42,270
worth of flops that they can allocate,

334
00:15:42,270 --> 00:15:45,430
and they want to how should we spend that billion dollars.

335
00:15:45,430 --> 00:15:47,010
It's measured in flops, not dollars.

336
00:15:47,010 --> 00:15:49,177
So again, there's a lot of different transformations

337
00:15:49,177 --> 00:15:52,070
you'd want to apply in order to really ground this out

338
00:15:52,070 --> 00:15:53,210
in raw economics.

339
00:15:53,210 --> 00:15:56,590
But flops is the one that this paper looked at.

340
00:15:56,590 --> 00:15:59,970
So they trained on some data sets of webtext.

341
00:15:59,970 --> 00:16:02,310
This is just next-character prediction.

342
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:05,030
And they varied a lot of different properties

343
00:16:05,030 --> 00:16:07,350
of the model, like the model size, how many parameters

344
00:16:07,350 --> 00:16:10,350
does it have, the data set size, so D, how many tokens

345
00:16:10,350 --> 00:16:12,270
are you training it on.

346
00:16:12,270 --> 00:16:14,983
And they also tried other things like the batch size--

347
00:16:14,983 --> 00:16:17,150
we'll come back to that at the end of this lecture--

348
00:16:17,150 --> 00:16:19,233
the shape of the network, what's the aspect ratio,

349
00:16:19,233 --> 00:16:23,590
like the depth compared to the width of the network.

350
00:16:23,590 --> 00:16:25,290
So they looked at all of these factors.

351
00:16:25,290 --> 00:16:26,910
It's a really rich paper.

352
00:16:26,910 --> 00:16:28,910
So we assign this as a reading because it's just

353
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:31,830
a beautiful piece of science and full of insight.

354
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:34,110
A lot of the findings have been modified

355
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:37,330
since-- they found their empirical fits to the data,

356
00:16:37,330 --> 00:16:39,890
and there's better empirical fits in recent years.

357
00:16:39,890 --> 00:16:44,150
But it's a good lesson in how to do this kind of work,

358
00:16:44,150 --> 00:16:48,290
and there's still a lot of great insights in the paper.

359
00:16:48,290 --> 00:16:49,850
So here's how they computed.

360
00:16:49,850 --> 00:16:51,690
Here's how they estimated compute.

361
00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:54,890
So I said that compute should be something

362
00:16:54,890 --> 00:16:58,730
like number of parameters times number of data points.

363
00:16:58,730 --> 00:17:03,010
So they used that as their rough approximation to compute.

364
00:17:03,010 --> 00:17:05,609
Again, this is just an approximation to how GPUs

365
00:17:05,609 --> 00:17:10,170
actually run because it's going to depend on things like

366
00:17:10,170 --> 00:17:13,210
what's the precision of-- is it a 16-bit number?

367
00:17:13,210 --> 00:17:15,349
These things, there's a lot of details that matter.

368
00:17:15,349 --> 00:17:17,849
But basically, compute equals number

369
00:17:17,849 --> 00:17:21,246
of parameters times number of data points observed.

370
00:17:21,246 --> 00:17:23,329
So number of data points is batch size times steps

371
00:17:23,329 --> 00:17:24,162
of gradient descent.

372
00:17:24,162 --> 00:17:27,569
So that's batch size times steps of gradient descent.

373
00:17:27,569 --> 00:17:30,110
And then times parameters, that's like how many flops.

374
00:17:30,110 --> 00:17:31,970
And then for some reason, you multiply by 6.

375
00:17:31,970 --> 00:17:33,850
I don't quite know where that comes from.

376
00:17:33,850 --> 00:17:39,070
But that's the constant that turns the units into flops.

377
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:44,740
So they ran these for petaflop days.

378
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:46,280
They ran this on a lot of compute.

379
00:17:49,420 --> 00:17:51,340
So the main finding of this paper

380
00:17:51,340 --> 00:17:55,540
is that there is a power law relationship between the test

381
00:17:55,540 --> 00:17:59,260
loss and the resources, either the number of parameters

382
00:17:59,260 --> 00:18:01,360
or the number of data points.

383
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:04,860
So resource X, again, that could be data or parameters

384
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:09,740
or even compute in test loss are related

385
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:14,860
by this type of power law, which is a 1

386
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:18,955
over the resource value to the alpha exponent.

387
00:18:18,955 --> 00:18:20,580
So alpha is a constant that will depend

388
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:25,120
on other properties, properties of the data, the world,

389
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:27,260
the task, the architecture.

390
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:30,020
But for a particular architecture or particular task

391
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:32,340
like language modeling with a transformer,

392
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:34,980
then we'll have some alpha that can be estimated,

393
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:38,660
and that's the parameter of the power law.

394
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:40,200
So what does a power law say?

395
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:43,280
It basically says if I double my resources,

396
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:44,860
I double my number of data points,

397
00:18:44,860 --> 00:18:49,680
my loss should go down by--

398
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:54,320
if I double, my loss should go down by 2 to the power.

399
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:56,000
Alpha is the parameter of the power law

400
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,720
that describes its shape.

401
00:18:57,720 --> 00:19:00,680
If I quadruple my data points, my loss

402
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:05,360
should go down by 4 to the negative alpha.

403
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:08,400
So let's say that alpha is 1, just for simplicity.

404
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:11,280
Then it's like if I double my number of data points

405
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:13,445
I train on, then my loss will go down by half.

406
00:19:13,445 --> 00:19:15,820
So 2 to the negative 1 will be my loss goes down by half.

407
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:21,680
If I quadruple, my loss goes down by 4.

408
00:19:21,680 --> 00:19:23,780
So my loss becomes a fourth.

409
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:26,400
So that's the idea of a power law.

410
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:34,380
If I scale my resource, I will scale my loss.

411
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:43,743
So if alpha is 2, then if I double my resource,

412
00:19:43,743 --> 00:19:45,160
then I'll get 2 to the negative 2.

413
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:48,880
So my loss will go down by one fourth of its previous value.

414
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:56,280
So here's the empirical fit that they found.

415
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:59,040
So on the y-axis is going to be the test loss.

416
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:01,928
And on the x-axis is going to be the number of tokens

417
00:20:01,928 --> 00:20:02,720
that they train on.

418
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:04,180
So that's the data set size.

419
00:20:04,180 --> 00:20:06,440
Remember, this is autoregressive transformer,

420
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:09,178
so tokens is we tokenize the words,

421
00:20:09,178 --> 00:20:10,720
and we're just predicting next token.

422
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:13,540
So this is how many tokens we predict.

423
00:20:13,540 --> 00:20:18,380
One important point here is that we're not

424
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:20,880
taking into account multi-epoch training.

425
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:22,980
So remember, I could have a data set of some size

426
00:20:22,980 --> 00:20:24,720
but revisit data points multiple times.

427
00:20:24,720 --> 00:20:26,057
So what does that count on?

428
00:20:26,057 --> 00:20:27,640
How do I measure the number of tokens?

429
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:29,880
This is all one epoch training, so it's very easy.

430
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:33,100
It's just I visit each data point exactly once.

431
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:36,820
And so the number of tokens that are processed

432
00:20:36,820 --> 00:20:42,040
is exactly equal to the number of tokens in my data set.

433
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:44,360
So this is a log-log plot.

434
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:46,600
So a power law on a log-log plot looks

435
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:48,440
like a line with a slope, which is

436
00:20:48,440 --> 00:20:53,360
equal to the exponent of the power law.

437
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:56,480
So there's a great fit.

438
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:00,480
That was the first main finding, that, empirically, there

439
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:03,360
exists a power law relationship between data set size and test

440
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:05,720
loss.

441
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:07,340
So what are the equations down here?

442
00:21:07,340 --> 00:21:11,600
So this is the test loss as a function of the data set size.

443
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:13,020
We're going to have data set size.

444
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:15,840
It'll be 1 over data set size to the alpha.

445
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:21,160
And alpha here, it turns out, empirically, to be about 0.1.

446
00:21:21,160 --> 00:21:24,168
And they also have this Dc, which is some constant.

447
00:21:24,168 --> 00:21:25,960
This is another parameter of the power law.

448
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:29,160
That just is going to change the offset of this line.

449
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:31,720
So think about take a log-log plot

450
00:21:31,720 --> 00:21:34,600
is like take a log of this side and a log of that side, that's

451
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:37,320
what we're plotting, one against the other.

452
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:39,810
If we take a log of this side, then the exponent

453
00:21:39,810 --> 00:21:41,610
just becomes we multiply--

454
00:21:41,610 --> 00:21:44,850
it's like the slope of log of what's

455
00:21:44,850 --> 00:21:49,130
on the inside and log of this over that

456
00:21:49,130 --> 00:21:51,533
is log of this minus that.

457
00:21:51,533 --> 00:21:52,950
So this is just a constant offset.

458
00:21:52,950 --> 00:21:55,530
That's like an offset on the y-axis.

459
00:21:55,530 --> 00:21:57,570
This is the y-intercept.

460
00:21:57,570 --> 00:21:58,802
Yeah, question.

461
00:21:58,802 --> 00:22:01,010
AUDIENCE: Is the form of the loss function important?

462
00:22:01,010 --> 00:22:03,365
Should I understand the loss function itself?

463
00:22:03,365 --> 00:22:04,490
It doesn't matter too much?

464
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:07,073
PHILLIP ISOLA: The form of the loss function, is it important?

465
00:22:07,073 --> 00:22:07,757
It is.

466
00:22:07,757 --> 00:22:10,090
And I think people have observed power laws with respect

467
00:22:10,090 --> 00:22:14,130
to both cross-entropy as well as mean squared error,

468
00:22:14,130 --> 00:22:17,510
different loss functions will result in different power laws.

469
00:22:17,510 --> 00:22:20,435
But they can be explained by power laws.

470
00:22:20,435 --> 00:22:22,810
In this case, I think we're looking at cross-entropy type

471
00:22:22,810 --> 00:22:25,410
loss.

472
00:22:25,410 --> 00:22:27,690
So next-word prediction, like cross-entropy.

473
00:22:27,690 --> 00:22:28,970
Yeah?

474
00:22:28,970 --> 00:22:31,290
AUDIENCE: So there was [INAUDIBLE]

475
00:22:31,290 --> 00:22:35,610
of the maximum, essentially, attention length or the number

476
00:22:35,610 --> 00:22:39,070
of tokens [INAUDIBLE].

477
00:22:39,070 --> 00:22:42,992
How does that relate to the [INAUDIBLE] here?

478
00:22:42,992 --> 00:22:44,950
PHILLIP ISOLA: So how does the attention length

479
00:22:44,950 --> 00:22:48,353
relate to this plot?

480
00:22:48,353 --> 00:22:49,770
That's not really showing up here.

481
00:22:49,770 --> 00:22:53,270
I think they're choosing some fixed attention, context length.

482
00:22:53,270 --> 00:22:55,910
You could also derive scaling laws with respect

483
00:22:55,910 --> 00:22:56,850
to context length.

484
00:22:56,850 --> 00:22:58,600
I believe people have looked at that, too.

485
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:01,410
So as I scale my context, how does performance change?

486
00:23:01,410 --> 00:23:03,590
Can I predictably say that if I want

487
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:08,590
performance to be 99% accurate, what's the context I need?

488
00:23:08,590 --> 00:23:10,630
There probably are scaling laws like this.

489
00:23:10,630 --> 00:23:12,463
I can't think of any off the top of my head,

490
00:23:12,463 --> 00:23:14,297
but that's not what they're looking at here.

491
00:23:14,297 --> 00:23:16,470
So I believe they're either choosing a fixed context

492
00:23:16,470 --> 00:23:18,710
length for all the models they're comparing,

493
00:23:18,710 --> 00:23:21,150
or maybe in some subsection of the paper,

494
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:22,670
they try different context lengths.

495
00:23:22,670 --> 00:23:23,570
I don't remember.

496
00:23:26,820 --> 00:23:28,618
So all of these things are simplifications.

497
00:23:28,618 --> 00:23:30,910
They're saying let's pick one setting, this transformer

498
00:23:30,910 --> 00:23:33,330
with this context length with cross-entropy loss,

499
00:23:33,330 --> 00:23:37,210
and let's measure how it scales with respect to one

500
00:23:37,210 --> 00:23:38,530
interesting parameter.

501
00:23:38,530 --> 00:23:44,530
And will that hold for an RNN with an MSE loss?

502
00:23:44,530 --> 00:23:45,390
Maybe, maybe not.

503
00:23:45,390 --> 00:23:47,723
So you should always keep that in the back of your mind.

504
00:23:47,723 --> 00:23:50,190
This is characterizing one popular family of models.

505
00:23:50,190 --> 00:23:52,390
But there are other models outside that family,

506
00:23:52,390 --> 00:23:57,130
and we don't yet know how well these laws will extrapolate.

507
00:23:57,130 --> 00:23:58,210
Let's go here.

508
00:23:58,210 --> 00:24:00,690
AUDIENCE: Is there any empirical interpretation

509
00:24:00,690 --> 00:24:02,530
of the value of DC?

510
00:24:02,530 --> 00:24:05,437
Does it represent actually something meaningful?

511
00:24:05,437 --> 00:24:06,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

512
00:24:06,270 --> 00:24:08,550
Does DC represent something meaningful?

513
00:24:08,550 --> 00:24:13,330
So I would say we have an equation with two

514
00:24:13,330 --> 00:24:16,810
free parameters, and we're trying to explain points

515
00:24:16,810 --> 00:24:18,030
with two free parameters.

516
00:24:18,030 --> 00:24:20,290
And we say that a good explanation

