779
00:35:33,650 --> 00:35:35,410
to my data set size.

780
00:35:35,410 --> 00:35:38,250
So the implication is, essentially, I

781
00:35:38,250 --> 00:35:43,330
should train relatively big models on relatively small data.

782
00:35:43,330 --> 00:35:45,570
So I should train a billion-size parameter model

783
00:35:45,570 --> 00:35:47,830
on a million-size data set.

784
00:35:47,830 --> 00:35:52,250
And that would be the best way to minimize my test

785
00:35:52,250 --> 00:35:53,510
loss according to this law.

786
00:35:58,495 --> 00:36:02,230
So people have studied this in a lot of different contexts.

787
00:36:02,230 --> 00:36:05,383
And I just want to give a flavor of the other power

788
00:36:05,383 --> 00:36:06,550
laws that people have found.

789
00:36:06,550 --> 00:36:09,170
So it's not just about autoregressive transformers.

790
00:36:09,170 --> 00:36:11,650
These power laws have showed up in computer vision,

791
00:36:11,650 --> 00:36:14,250
in reinforcement learning, in a lot of areas.

792
00:36:14,250 --> 00:36:16,930
Here's a paper on vision power laws.

793
00:36:16,930 --> 00:36:22,230
So they are fitting, I think, a vision transformer to do--

794
00:36:22,230 --> 00:36:24,148
or a computer vision model.

795
00:36:24,148 --> 00:36:26,190
I think they might have tried a few architectures

796
00:36:26,190 --> 00:36:30,270
to do CIFAR-10 classification or some other type

797
00:36:30,270 --> 00:36:32,310
of classification problem.

798
00:36:32,310 --> 00:36:35,350
And here, they're plotting the power law in two dimensions.

799
00:36:35,350 --> 00:36:38,010
So we have our loss on the y-axis.

800
00:36:38,010 --> 00:36:43,070
And we have our data set size and our model size

801
00:36:43,070 --> 00:36:45,150
on the two other axes.

802
00:36:45,150 --> 00:36:48,085
So this vertical axis is the loss.

803
00:36:48,085 --> 00:36:50,210
And there's kind of a power law in both dimensions,

804
00:36:50,210 --> 00:36:51,750
but they interact in a weird way.

805
00:36:51,750 --> 00:36:54,213
So again, it's not something that you can easily

806
00:36:54,213 --> 00:36:55,130
wrap your mind around.

807
00:36:55,130 --> 00:36:56,797
But we do have a simple functional form,

808
00:36:56,797 --> 00:36:59,710
which is just the sum of these two power laws.

809
00:36:59,710 --> 00:37:03,950
So same as they found in language modeling.

810
00:37:03,950 --> 00:37:06,653
One of the questions that you might

811
00:37:06,653 --> 00:37:08,070
have with this kind of reading tea

812
00:37:08,070 --> 00:37:13,790
leaves concern is, do these power laws actually

813
00:37:13,790 --> 00:37:15,910
make predictions that extrapolate

814
00:37:15,910 --> 00:37:17,490
across orders of magnitude?

815
00:37:17,490 --> 00:37:21,570
If I fit them on some subset of my data at some scale,

816
00:37:21,570 --> 00:37:23,680
do they make predictions that are

817
00:37:23,680 --> 00:37:27,200
valid on held-out data at larger scale?

818
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:29,000
And the answer is yes.

819
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,360
So in this paper, they ran this experiment

820
00:37:31,360 --> 00:37:35,280
where they fit the model on these green points.

821
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:39,120
So they have models of different size,

822
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:41,760
but they did this out-of-distribution held-out

823
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:42,260
test.

824
00:37:42,260 --> 00:37:44,360
So they fit the model on some scale.

825
00:37:44,360 --> 00:37:48,260
And then they scaled up the model to a larger scale,

826
00:37:48,260 --> 00:37:52,280
or in this case, it might be a smaller scale,

827
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:54,120
but you can do it in either direction.

828
00:37:54,120 --> 00:37:56,380
So you fit the model on these data points.

829
00:37:56,380 --> 00:37:58,760
And you ask, does it make valid predictions

830
00:37:58,760 --> 00:38:01,140
for models that are of a different scale?

831
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:02,120
And it does.

832
00:38:02,120 --> 00:38:04,083
So you can do cross-validation.

833
00:38:04,083 --> 00:38:05,500
We're not just reading tea leaves.

834
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:09,580
That's really all that this says.

835
00:38:13,280 --> 00:38:17,480
So finding one, we have a power law relationship

836
00:38:17,480 --> 00:38:20,680
between resources and performance.

837
00:38:20,680 --> 00:38:23,090
Finding two, the--
